 20	/// have no entry and fall back to a named-table read.
 21	///
 22	/// Collection fails closed: only the slice that maps faithfully onto a `parquet_scan` over
 23	/// local files is accepted — a `FILES()` query (not a load), reading local parquet files whose
 24	/// columns are direct passthroughs to the scan tuple. Byte ranges assigned to this instance are
 25	/// normalized per file: overlaps are refused (they would read rows twice), adjacent ranges
 26	/// coalesce, an exact tiling of the whole file collapses to a whole-file read, and anything
 27	/// else is emitted as explicit `[start, start+length)` splits — the engine reads exactly the
 28	/// row groups whose start offset falls inside each split, so the instances of a distributed
 29	/// scan together read every row exactly once. Anything outside that slice (loads, remote
 30	/// schemes, casts or other column transforms, path-derived or flexibly-mapped columns,
 31	/// incremental `has_more` delivery) is rejected with [`TranslateError::UnsupportedScanRange`]
 32	/// rather than silently producing wrong results.
